Simple Wound Dressing Change
General Practice Other
Scheduled wound management, Assessment of granulation tissue, Cleansing with normal saline, Application of appropriate dressing

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-042
Reason for Visit: Acute strike-through bleeding and severe pain related to a dislodged, heavily soiled surgical dressing on a vulnerable patient.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an elderly patient with a recent deep surgical wound whose heavy dressing had completely dislodged during a transfer, resulting in acute strike-through bleeding, exposure of the raw wound bed to the environment, and severe localized pain. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to physically evaluate the compromised wound for dehiscence, aggressively cleanse the contaminated site, and apply a specialized secure dressing. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the deep wound entirely exposed overnight, risking massive bacterial contamination and unbearable pain.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My bandage got ripped off when they moved me, it's bleeding all over the sheets, and the raw skin is burning terribly."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents for what should be a scheduled presentation for the ongoing management of a healing acute surgical wound (skin graft donor site), but is experiencing an acute complication due to dressing failure. Following the traumatic dislodgment of the dressing one hour ago, there was sudden, moderate bleeding and severe pain upon exposure to the air. The patient requires the careful removal of the previous soiled dressing fragments and an urgent assessment of the wound bed for healthy granulation tissue or slough. The patient complains of a severe, stinging pain over the entire raw surface area. There is a complete absence of severe systemic inflammatory signs or high fever prior to this event.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to the acute pain from the exposed wound.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of iatrogenic infection due to the exposed, vulnerable surgical site in a facility setting.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, wincing, clutching the bedrails to avoid moving the affected leg.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: The right anterior thigh (donor site) reveals a 10cm x 10cm raw, oozing surface. The assessment of granulation tissue confirms a healthy, pink, pinpoint bleeding bed without significant slough or necrotic tissue. The evaluation of the surrounding skin shows mild localized erythema consistent with normal healing, without maceration or spreading cellulitis. The clinical intervention requires gentle cleansing of the wound utilizing normal saline and the application of an appropriate primary dressing. Expected investigation results include a steady reduction in wound dimensions measured serially over time without positive pathogenic growth on targeted wound swab cultures (swab taken tonight due to environmental exposure).
Environmental Observations: Blood-stained bed linens; facility staff actively assisting with patient positioning.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 1g given with minimal relief; verified.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Simple Wound Dressing Change (Complicated by Acute Traumatic Dislodgment) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Wound Dehiscence - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The wound is a superficial skin graft donor site, not a deep primary closure involving fascia or muscle layers; the bleeding is strictly capillary oozing from the raw dermis.

Acute Wound Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of purulent, foul-smelling exudate, lack of systemic fever, and the sudden, traumatic onset of the pain and bleeding rather than a gradual infectious progression.

Arterial Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bleeding is widespread, uniform, and venous/capillary in nature, entirely lacking pulsatile flow.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Infection Risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a traumatic loss of a critical surgical dressing, resulting in acute pain and the direct environmental exposure of a massive, raw wound bed. Telephone triage correctly identified the high risk of nosocomial infection and the immediate need for pain management through wound coverage. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was clinically mandated to sterilely irrigate the contaminated wound, rule out deep tissue trauma, and apply a specialized, highly adherent secondary dressing to seal the nerve endings and arrest the capillary bleeding. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have subjected the patient to excruciating, unremitting pain and a near-certain deep tissue infection overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Performed gentle cleansing of the wound utilizing warmed normal saline to remove clotted blood and environmental debris. Collected a baseline wound swab for M/C/S due to the exposure. Applied a non-adherent silicone contact layer followed by an appropriate primary dressing (absorbent foam). Secured the dressing firmly with secondary retention tape and a tubular bandage to prevent future shear-force dislodgment.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent future traumatic dressing failures during bed transfers, strictly instruct the patient to physically place both hands flat on their chest whenever being moved by the hoists or staff. By locking the arms into a central, predictable position, you eliminate the frantic reaching or catching motions that frequently snag on bedrails and tear off secure bandages.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Facility nursing directive updated regarding secure transfer techniques.
DME/Safety (Optional): Specialized foam dressings and tubular bandages (utilized from bag).
Follow-up: Facility GP or practice nurse review in 3 days for the next scheduled dressing change and to review the swab results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Facility staff to arrange immediate ED transfer via 000 if the patient develops a sudden, high-spiking fever with uncontrollable shivering (rigors), or if massive, bright red blood rapidly soaks through the newly applied heavy foam dressing.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The sharp, burning pain is significantly reduced now that the raw nerve endings are sealed away from the air. The wound will continue to ooze slightly over the next few days, which the thick foam dressing will absorb safely.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Alert the nursing staff immediately if the severe, burning pain returns, as this means the dressing has slipped again.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Ripping off the bandage exposed all the tiny nerve endings in your healing skin to the cold air, which causes severe burning pain. We have washed away the dirt and applied a thick, protective foam cushion that acts like a second skin to stop the pain and the bleeding.
Actionable Steps: Keep your hands securely on your chest when the nurses are turning you. Do not attempt to adjust or touch the new bandage yourself.

